# Atlas.ti Backup Sync — Fallback Workflow Only

> Primary workflow is now QDPX-first via `.cursor/skills/qdpx/`.
> Apply this rule only when running direct SQLite scripts in `.cursor/skills/atlasti/`.

The live Atlas.ti project database lives **outside** this workspace:

```
C:\Users\dtlut\AppData\Roaming\Scientific Software\ATLASti.25\Libraries25\
```

This folder has its own git repo with remote `origin` → `https://github.com/Luttik/atlas-thesis-backup` (private).

## Startup hygiene (mandatory)

Before opening Atlas.ti after any restore or external DB write:

1. Check for stale `*_WC_*.sqlite` files in the project folder.
2. Check for stale `Library.lock` in `Libraries25`.

Do not delete either file automatically. Ask the researcher first and confirm a backup commit exists in `Libraries25` before deletion.

These stale files can block project loading even if the main `.sqlite` passes integrity checks.

## Rule (SQLite fallback only)

**Before augmenting any Atlas.ti data with AI** (editing codes, memos, quotations, or running import/export scripts), always ensure the latest Atlas.ti files are pushed to the backup repo.

Treat this as a hard preflight gate: no DB write operations until push succeeds (or there are provably no changes and branch is already up to date).

Enforcement sequence:
1. Check `git status --short --branch` in `Libraries25`.
2. Run `git pull --rebase`.
3. If there are unsynced changes, create a local sync commit first.
4. Attempt `git push`.
5. Only then run augmentation scripts.
6. For quote writes, run `atlasti_export.py --dry-run` before the real export (strict self-check is default-on).
7. After writes complete, commit and push `Libraries25` again.

Run this first:

```powershell
cd "C:\Users\dtlut\AppData\Roaming\Scientific Software\ATLASti.25\Libraries25"
git status --short --branch
git pull --rebase
git add .
git commit -m "Sync before AI augmentation $(Get-Date -Format 'yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm')"
git push
```

If there is nothing to commit, confirm with `git status` that the working tree is clean before proceeding.

For export operations, strict self-check is default-on:

```powershell
python .cursor/skills/atlasti/atlasti_export.py --dry-run
python .cursor/skills/atlasti/atlasti_export.py
```

Only for explicit one-off overrides, allow `--skip-strict-self-check`.

After export, create and push a post-write snapshot commit in `Libraries25`.

After quotation operations are applied, run a mandatory integrity check:

```powershell
python .cursor/skills/atlasti/verify_quote_ops.py --ops <ops-file>.json
```

Do not finish a coding run unless verification passes (`verified operations: N/N`, no errors).

After successful verification, run startup hygiene again before reopening Atlas.ti. If stale `*_WC_*.sqlite` or `Library.lock` are present, request researcher confirmation before deletion and only delete after backup commit confirmation.

> **Why**: Atlas.ti has no undo for external changes. A pushed backup before and after AI-driven edits ensures both rollback and auditability.
